The extension links libnixstore, libnixexpr and the Nix libraries they
rest on, as shared objects. That is a "work that uses the Library" in the
sense of LGPL section 5, and section 6 permits it under Apache-2.0. The
source of every part that is not Nix is the huggorm repository
(https://github.com/nixidae/huggorm), so a user can rebuild the extension
against a Nix of their own.

The manylinux wheel bundles those Nix libraries, built from the Nix
source that `nix/versions.nix` pins, with the patches in `nix/patches`.
That source and those patches are the source of the bundled libraries.

`get-env.sh` is Nix's own script, `src/nix/get-env.sh` in the Nix
source this package links. Nix compiles it into the `nix` binary,
where no library can reach it, so a copy travels beside the bindings,
from the Nix of the same version. The copy is the text the binary
embeds: the file, after the one newline Nix's build puts before it. It
is LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the rest of Nix is.
